<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Satyashodhak Samaj</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1873">1873</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Truth-seekers' Society</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Satyashodhak Samaj, the Truth-seekers' Society, was a social reform movement founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> in 1873 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jyotirao-phule">Jyotirao Phule</a>. It campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> discrimination and the religious and social dominance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, and for the education of lower castes and women. In the early 20th century it was strongly supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahu-of-kolhapur">Shahu of Kolhapur</a> and fed the non-Brahmin movement in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/satyashodhak-samaj.jpg" alt="Satyashodhak Samaj" width="250" height="354" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mphule.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jyotirao-phule">Jyotirao Phule</a> founded the society on 24 September 1873. Born into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> family in 1827 and educated at a Christian missionary school, he had been drawn to the Arya Samaj, the Prarthana Samaj and the Poona Sarvajanik Sabha, but doubted that they would win rights for the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>. His wife Savitribai headed the society's women's section. The Samaj held to one God, taught the equality of all people as given by a creator, and rejected priests as intermediaries and the caste system.</p><p>It argued that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a> had been manufactured to protect priestly standing, which gave the low <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> the right to contest them. It urged cheaper weddings, inter-caste marriage, an end to child marriage and the right of widows to remarry, and stressed English education. From 1877 to 1897 it published the newspaper Deenbandhu. It looked to the British government as the power most likely to treat the low castes fairly, which set it against the nationalist groups of the time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Finding lectures ineffective in the countryside, the Samaj turned to tamashas, folk dramas that kept their traditional form but mocked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> superiority, beginning with an invocation to Ganpati read as gan, people, and pati, leader. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satara">Satara</a> in 1919 tenants revolted against their Brahmin landlords. Critics such as Vishnushashtri Chiplunkar called its work sacrilegious and anti-nationalist and accused it of courting the colonial government.</p><p>The movement languished after Phule's death and was revived by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahu-of-kolhapur">Shahu Maharaj</a> of Kolhapur, who in 1902 reserved 50 percent of civil service posts in his state for communities other than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, Prabhus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsi</a>. By the 1920s it had strong roots in Western Maharashtra and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidarbha">Vidarbha</a> and spoke for the Bahujan Samaj. In the 1930s leaders such as Keshavrao Jedhe joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>, and the Samaj withered away.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Its doctrines of universal rights and equality and its case against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> dominance of social, religious and political life left a lasting mark on Indian politics. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalit">Dalit</a> political movement, distinct from the non-Brahmin movement, continued to draw on Phule's teachings into the early 20th century. Later followers included the educationalist Bhaurao Patil and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> leaders such as Nana Patil and Khanderao Bagal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Satyashodhak Samaj” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shahu, who reigned from 1894 to 1922, founded schools and hostels, reserved posts for non-Brahmin and backward castes in 1902, and supported the <b>Satyashodhak</b> movement.</blockquote><a href="/state/kolhapur-state">Kolhapur State</a></li></ul></section>
